# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "The operator after a named run prefix reads as an operator"

# RULED 2026-09-21: `:sort[title]` — the shape an author reaches for after a NAMED filter run
# prefix (`:sort`, `:filter`, `:map`, `:reduce` …) when they mean `:sort[title[]]` and forget the
# operand brackets — left `title` uncoloured, carrying only the run's own contentName, where
# `[sort[title]]`'s "sort" (a genuine operator WITH an operand) reads
# `keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5`. filters.js#parseFilterOperation actually THROWS
# "Missing [ in filter expression" on `:sort[title]` at runtime — the operand bracket is not
# optional — but graceful parsing paints what the author typed the operator colour every other
# run's operator reads, rather than the colour of plain content.
#
# The fix must stay narrow: it must never claim a bare QUOTED operand for the empty/default
# operator (`["Not Legal"]`, equally malformed but a STRING, never a name) or trailing whitespace
# ahead of a multi-line operand's own closing `]`.

! The control: an operator WITH an operand reads as an operator, prefixed or not

{{{ [sort[title]] }}}
#    ^^^^ meta.filter.operator.step.tiddlywiki5 keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5

{{{ :subfilter[tag[a]] }}}
#              ^^^ meta.filter.operator.step.tiddlywiki5 keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5

! The red case: a named prefix's bare operator, no operand at all, now reads the same way

{{{ :sort[title] }}}
#         ^^^^^ meta.filter.operator.step.tiddlywiki5 keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5

! A quoted operand for the empty/default operator is a STRING, never claimed as an operator name

{{{ :test["Not Legal"] }}}
#         ^ - keyword.operator.operator.filter.tiddlywiki5
#         ^ entity.filter.operator.title.tiddlywiki5
